# ~/.bash_profile -- login shell entry point. Deliberately a stub.
#
# ASCII ONLY, comments included -- see the note in ~/.bashrc's header
# (enforced by check 1b in ~/.config/bash/selftest.sh).
#
# Everything real lives in ~/.bashrc, which is the single load-bearing shell
# config on this machine (see its header for the tier-1/tier-2 rule).
#
# WHY THIS IS EMPTY:
# This file used to hold PATH entries and env vars "because they're only
# needed at login". That was never true -- Claude Code's Bash tool, launchd
# agents, and cron get their environment from BASH_ENV (-> ~/.bashrc) or
# from inheritance, never from here. The split created a third place to
# guess wrong about and produced two real bugs. Do not repopulate it:
# new PATH/env additions go in ~/.bashrc's tier 1, machine-local ones in
# ~/.bashrc.local.
#
# WATCH OUT: installers append `export PATH=...` to the END of this file.
# Because that runs after the source line below, such a line silently
# outranks every deliberate ordering decision in ~/.bashrc. (rustup did
# exactly this.) Nothing can prevent that -- but ~/.config/bash/selftest.sh
# asserts the expected head of PATH and the Claude Code session-start hook
# runs it, so a stray append gets caught within one session instead of
# lingering. If you find one below, move it into ~/.bashrc's tier 1
# __path_prepend list where its priority is explicit, and delete it here.
#
# TAMPER ALARM: any drift of this file from its blessed copy
# (~/.config/bash/bash_profile.expected) triggers a loud red warning in
# every new interactive shell -- see the alarm block at the top of
# ~/.bashrc tier 2. After a DELIBERATE edit here, re-bless with:
#     cp ~/.bash_profile ~/.config/bash/bash_profile.expected

[[ -f ~/.bashrc ]] && source ~/.bashrc

# Hermes Agent - ensure ~/.local/bin is on PATH
